  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8{e}^{t},3\\sqrt{t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3{t}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{t}+{C}_{1},\\,2t\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=\u27e81,0\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\u27e8{e}^{0}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)\\sqrt{0}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e81,0\u27e9 & =\u27e81+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8{e}^{t},\\,2t\\sqrt{t}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =\u27e8{e}^{4},\\,2(4)\\sqrt{4}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{4},\\,16\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
